   MANAGEMENT},
Year = {1997},
Pages = {41-47},
Note = {XXVII International-Association-of-Hydrogeologists Congress, E MIDLANDS
   CONF CTR, NOTTINGHAM, ENGLAND, SEP 22-26, 1997},
Organization = {Int Assoc Hydrogeologists; British Geol Survey, Hydrogeol Grp; Golder
   Associates; Geol Soc London, Hydrogeol Grp; Overseas Dev Adm; UNESCO,
   Div Water Sci; WHO UNEP Global Environm Monitoring Syst; Severn Trent
   Water; Entec Europe Ltd; NIREX UK Ltd; Environm Agcy},
Abstract = {Urban groundwater is under-used in the UK, mainly because of worries
   about the risks of pollution. Geotechnical problems have arisen as a
   consequence of rising water tables. In order to make full and wise use
   of the asset value, a clear understanding of the quantities of
   groundwater recharge is needed. Recharge sources are varied and complex
   in cities, with major changes to the hydrological pathways for
   precipitation, some of which can increase recharge compared to rural
   areas. In addition, much of the sizeable imports of water for public
   supplies finds its way to groundwater, through leaking mains and sewers,
   or by deliberate recharge in septic tanks and soakaways.},
