Note = {2nd International Conference on Behavioral, Cognitive and Psychological
   Sciences (BCPS 2011), MALDIVES, NOV 25-26, 2011},
Organization = {ISI Proceedings; IEDRC; EBSCO Informat Serv},
Abstract = {The current research aims at the way a certain dose of caffeine and
   energy drinks consumption influences the reaction time for the
   peripheral visual stimuli and the number of correct answers and errors
   to of visual stimuli from the central field. The participants were 162
   students at the Faculty of Psychology and Education Sciences, 31 males
   and 131 females, aged between 18 and 25 years old (M = 20.73; S.D =
   1.74), rural and urban areas. The instruments were the peripheral
   perception test and Dest test from Vienna Tests System. The results
   obtained highlight the fact that consuming a quantity of 165 mg of
   caffeine increases the speed reaction to visual stimuli, the reaction
   time statistically significant decreasing (p<0.05), the correct speed
   and distance estimation of visual stimuli being statistically
   significant low (p<0.05) both for the peripheral visual stimuli and for
   the ones from the central field. Moreover, the consumption of energy
   drinks (2000 mg taurine) has a reduction effect of the correct
   estimation concerning the visual stimuli's speed of movement in the
   central field (p<0.05). The conclusion of the research emphasizes the
   fact that caffeine and energy drinks consumption reduces the reaction
   time but increases the number of incorrect speed of movement estimations
   from the central and peripheral field.},
