   Society-for-Industrial-and-Organizational-Psychology, SAN DIEGO, CA, APR
   27-29, 2001},
Organization = {Soc Ind \& Org Psychol},
Abstract = {Two hundred forty undergraduates rated their perceptions of an
   individual for a job requiring relocation using a 2 x 3 (Gender: Single
   Male, Single Female x Child: No Child, 3-Year-Old, 6-Year-Old)
   between-subjects experimental design. Findings indicate that single
   parents are perceived as having a more difficult time adjusting to a
   move but are also perceived to be more mature than childless singles.
   Single parents are also more likely to be offered a job that did not
   require relocation and are more likely to be awarded a merit-based
   stipend than childless singles. Support was also found for the
   hypothesized mediating role of perceptions of adjustment and maturity in
   understanding actions taken toward single parents. The results are
   discussed in terms of implications for future research and applied
   practice.},
