Note = {5th International Workshop on Energy and Environment of Residential
   Buildings/3rd International Conference on Built Environment and Public
   Health, Guilin, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 29-31, 2009},
Organization = {Hunan Univ; Univ Hong Kong; Tsinghua Univ},
Abstract = {The ventilation in the urban city has been increasingly considered as
   one of the most effective methods for the heat and pollutant removal.
   The temperature differences between the urban surfaces and atmospheric
   air can also play an important role on the local air movement in the
   dense city under the calm conditions. The objective of this paper is to
   investigate the thermal buoyancy-induced ventilation in the urban
   streets with a complex terrain including the mountain and sea with and
   without winds. A 3D RNG k-epsilon turbulent model was used for the
   Simulation. When there is no wind, the total ventilation flow rate is a
   function of thermal buoyancy force. The streets perpendicular to the
   mountain can be used as a ventilation path for the city. When there is
   wind, a strong wind is needed to remove the heat and pollution in the
   long street at the mountain foot. To achieve better urban ventilation,
   the mountain-sea ``ventilation path{''} should be introduced in the
   urban area to avoid the ``long street effect{''}.},
