   together Engineering and Economics (ICEE), Guimaraes, PORTUGAL, JUN
   18-19, 2015},
Organization = {Univ Minho, Sch Engn; Univ Porto, Sch Econ \& Management},
Abstract = {Emerging markets have a crucial importance in the worldwide economy, but
   sometimes they are wrongly associated with depreciative social and
   environmental practices. Still, there are companies playing in these
   markets, which thoroughly incorporate these issues in their strategies
   and can represent a performance model to the global world. In this paper
   we use a case study approach to analyse how Duratex S.A. (Duratex), a
   film from an emerging market with a solid reputation for corporate
   sustainability (CS) leadership, manages its relations with its employees
   and the way these perceive and engage with the firm's sustainability
   activities. We aim at understanding whether a company with such
   characteristics deems its workforce as important as its external
   stakeholders when developing and communicating its sustainability
   activities, as well as evaluating its workforce's recognition of and
   engagement with such activities. It is also important for us to
   understand if sustainability is a reality for the majority of the
   employees or only a concept known by the top management and related
   positions. Results suggest that Duratex does attribute significant
   importance to its workforce and that its employees have a good knowledge
   of its sustainability practices and engage with them.},
