   JUN 25-27, 2024},
Organization = {University of Salento},
Abstract = {This study examines the European Union's (EU) strategies to mitigate
   climate change and develop smart cities, with a particular focus on the
   integration of environmental policies into sustainable urban
   environments. This study examines the relationship between climate
   change and the smart city model in the European context, emphasising the
   crucial need for cities to implement preventive strategies to reduce
   harmful emissions and mitigate climate change, with the objective of
   enhancing community resilience. The strategic vision of the smart city
   initiative is designed to provide an effective response to the
   complexities posed by such environmental issues. The EU provides support
   for smart city ecosystems through the implementation of innovative
   funding and policy initiatives, with the objective of achieving
   long-term sustainability and an enhanced quality of life. Notable
   initiatives include Horizon Europe, a research and innovation funding
   programme, and the Smart City Marketplace, which fosters collaboration
   between cities, industry, and researchers. The European Commission's
   European Mission of Smart and Climate-Neutral Cities (2021-2027) has the
   objective of guiding 100 EU cities towards climate neutrality by 2030,
   in alignment with the United Nations' Sustainable Development Goals,
   Agenda 2030. The Turin 2030 Action Plan serves as a case study,
   exemplifying the promotion of sustainable and resilient urban
   development through the implementation of citizen participation,
   cultural promotion, and strategies for the establishment of a more
   accessible, circular, and green city model.},
