   (The Sustainable City), Skiathos Isl, GREECE, SEP 24-26, 2008},
Organization = {Univ Thessaly; Wessex Inst Technol; Univ Siena; WIT Transact Ecol \&
   Environm},
Abstract = {In 2005, Mexico City introduced MetroBus, a single BRT line along
   Avienda de Insurgentes, the main north-south artery through the capital.
   The line has been well received, attracting more than a quarter million
   riders every weekday. This single line has connections to suburban
   trains, the metro system and both the public and private bus systems.
   This paper reports on a survey of BRT passengers that focused on how
   MetroBus trips have been integrated with other transportation modes, how
   the cost of MetroBus compared with total trip costs, general service
   quality, and desired new routes. It also highlights how BRT can be used
   in conjunction with other mass transit options to facilitate travel in
   even the most congested cities, contributing to a more sustainable urban
   environment.},
